{
 "summary": "For a smooth, periodic family f and a fixed parameter p, the Lean theorem bounds every iterated coordinate derivative (any word w of length m) of slice (inverse d f) p, at any point Y, by mixedLossConstant m · C. Here C bounds |f|, the pure x-derivative of order m+5, and the pure y-derivative of order m+5 on [0,1]². This is a C^m tame estimate for the torus inverse, matching the shape of (8.19). The main caveat is the derivative count: Lean loses 5 derivatives and uses only pure derivatives, while the paper states a loss of 4 measured in the full C^{m+4} norm. The existence, uniqueness and Fourier-series parts of Lemma 8.6 are not stated here.",
 "glossary": [
  {"decl": "partialX", "line": 4, "plain": "∂_x f(z) = Df(z)(1,0), the Fréchet derivative applied to the first coordinate direction.", "standard_concept": "first partial derivative", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "Its junk value is 0 where f is not differentiable, but f is smooth here."},
  {"decl": "xJet", "line": 6, "plain": "∂_x^p f, the p-th iterate of ∂_x.", "standard_concept": "pure higher x-derivative", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "derivativeWord", "line": 9, "plain": "For a word w ∈ {x,y}^m, the mixed derivative ∂_{w_1}⋯∂_{w_m} f.", "standard_concept": "mixed partial derivative of order m", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "Ranging over all w yields every derivative entering the C^m norm."},
  {"decl": "mixedLossConstant", "line": 14, "plain": "C_r = 6‖ω⁻¹‖‖ω‖^r · 3^{r+5} · Σ_{k∈Z²} weight(k)^{-4}.", "standard_concept": "the constant C_m in (8.19)", "matches_standard": "unclear", "note": "If Σ weight(k)^{-4} were not summable, the tsum would be 0 and the bound would degenerate to 0. Presumably weight(k) ~ 1+|k|, which makes the sum summable in 2D. The constant does not depend on d, and how the divisor bound (6.7) for v_t enters is not visible."}
 ],
 "lean_claims": [
  {"theorem": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 19, "plain": "Assume f : P × Plane → ℂ is C^∞ and periodic, p ∈ P, w is a derivative word of length m, Y ∈ Plane, and C ∈ ℝ satisfies three bounds on [0,1]²: |f(p,·)| ≤ C, |∂_x^{m+5} f(p,·)| ≤ C, and |∂_y^{m+5} f(p,·)| ≤ C (the last via swap). Then |∂_w (inverse d f)(p,Y)| ≤ C_m · C."}
 ],
 "paper_claims": [
  {"ref": "Lemma 8.6 (8.19)", "plain": "For smooth zero-mean F on T², Nφ = F has a unique smooth zero-mean solution N⁻¹F given by the Fourier series Σ_{k≠0} F̂(k)/(2πi v_t·k) e^{2πik·y}. It satisfies ‖N⁻¹F‖_{C^m} ≤ C_m‖F‖_{C^{m+4}} for every m ≥ 0."}
 ],
 "coverage": [
  {"paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6 (8.19) norm bound", "lean_theorem": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "relation": "different", "why": "Lean requires order m+5 pure x- and y-derivatives plus sup|f|; the paper requires the full C^{m+4} norm. Lean loses one more derivative but needs no mixed derivatives."},
  {"paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6 existence/uniqueness/Fourier formula", "lean_theorem": null, "relation": "not_formalized", "why": "Not stated in this declaration; it may live in the definition of `inverse` or in other lemmas."}
 ],
